"flex-wrap" is a CSS property that controls how flex items are laid out in a flex container. The value of the "flex-wrap" property determines whether flex items are forced onto one line or can wrap onto multiple lines.

When "flex-wrap" is set to "nowrap", flex items are forced onto one line and any remaining free space in the container is distributed proportionally among the items. This means that the items will stack on top of each other and fill the available space in a single line.

When "flex-wrap" is set to "wrap", flex items can wrap onto multiple lines. The available space is distributed proportionally among the items, and any extra space is distributed evenly among the items on each line. This means that the items will automatically overflow onto multiple lines if the container does not have enough space to fit them on a single line.

Here is an example of using the "flex-wrap" property:
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
```
In this example, the "container" element is set to display as a flex container and to allow flex items to wrap onto multiple lines using the "flex-wrap: wrap" property.

