Dynamic memory allocation is a process of allocating memory at runtime using the `malloc`, `calloc`, and `realloc` functions. These functions allow you to allocate memory of a specified size and store a pointer to the allocated memory in a variable.

Here are some examples of dynamic memory allocation in C:

1. Allocating memory for an array of integers:
```arduino
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *arr;
    int n = 10;

    // Allocate memory for an array of integers with size n
    arr = (int *) malloc(n * sizeof(int));

    // Check if memory allocation was successful
    if (arr == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Access the allocated memory
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = i * 2;
    }

    // Print the contents of the array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }

    // Free the allocated memory
    free(arr);

    return 0;
}
```
1. Allocating memory for a string:
```arduino
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char *str;
    int n = 10;

    // Allocate memory for a string with size n
    str = (char *) malloc(n * sizeof(char));

    // Check if memory allocation was successful
    if (str == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Assign values to the allocated memory
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        str[i] = 'A' + i;
    }

    // Print the contents of the string
