In SAS (Statistical Analysis System), `nodup` is an option used with the `PROC SORT` procedure to remove duplicate observations from a dataset. The `nodup` option checks for and removes observations where all variables are identical. If there are duplicate records in the dataset, only the first occurrence will be kept, and the duplicates will be removed.

Here's an example of how to use the `nodup` option with `PROC SORT`:

```sas
data example;
    input id name $ age;
    datalines;
1 John 25
2 Jane 30
3 John 25
4 Mary 28
5 Jane 30
;
run;

proc sort data=example out=example_nodup nodup;
    by id name age;
run;

proc print data=example_nodup;
run;
```

In this example, the `PROC SORT` procedure sorts the dataset `example` by the variables `id`, `name`, and `age`. The `nodup` option is used to remove any duplicate records with the same values for all three variables. The new dataset `example_nodup` contains only unique records, and the `PROC PRINT` statement prints the sorted dataset without duplicates.

