Here is a Python implementation of the dynamic programming approach:

```python
def dp(i, j, x):
    if i == j or i == 0 or j == 0:
        return 1
    elif i > j or j > i:
        return 0
    else:
        return dp(i-1, j-1, x) + dp(i-1, j, x-A[i-1]) + dp(i, j-1, x-A[j-1])

def solve(N, A, X):
    return dp(N, N, X)
```

The function dp is a recursive function that computes the number of square submatrices with a given sum. The base case is when i == j or i == 0 or j == 0, in which case there is only one square submatrix and its sum is 1.

The recursive case computes the number of square submatrices using the following rules:

    - If i > j or j > i, then there are no square submatrices that can be formed.
    - Else, the number of square submatrices is equal to the number of square submatrices that can be formed using the same row or column minus 1, plus the number of square submatrices that can be formed using the other row or column with a reduced sum.

The function solve calls dp with the appropriate arguments and returns the result.

This solution has a time complexity of O(N^3) and a space complexity of O(N^2) due to the use of the dynamic programming approach.

