1. Use the Argo CD API to manually trigger the cronjob. Argo CD exposes the /api/v1/cronjobs endpoint to manage cronjobs. You can make a POST request to /api/v1/cronjobs/{name}/run to manually trigger a cronjob run:

```bash
curl -X POST https://your-argo-server/api/v1/cronjobs/my-cronjob/run
```

2. Use the Argo CD UI to manually run the cronjob. In the Argo CD UI, you can go to the Cronjobs tab, select your cronjob, and click "Run Now" to manually trigger it.

![Argo CD Run Now Button](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/cronjob-run-now.png)

3. Increase the cron schedule frequency temporarily. You can patch the cronjob to have a more frequent schedule (e.g. every minute), wait for your manual run to trigger, then patch it back to the original schedule. For example:

```bash
# Patch to every minute 
curl -X PATCH https://your-argo-server/api/v1/cronjobs/my-cronjob -d '{
  "spec": { "schedule": "* * * * *" } 
}'

# Wait for manual run...

# Patch back to original schedule
curl -X PATCH https://your-argo-server/api/v1/cronjobs/my-cronjob -d '{
  "spec": { "schedule": "0 11 * * *" } 
}'
```

