In C++, a destructor is a special type of member function that is executed automatically when an object is destroyed. A destructor is defined using the `~` symbol before the function name, and it has no return type or parameters.

The purpose of a destructor is to clean up any resources that the object had allocated during its lifetime. For example, if an object had dynamically allocated memory using the `new` operator, the destructor can call the `delete` operator to free the memory.

Here's an example of how to define a destructor in C++:
```c++
class MyClass {
